#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Keep one Remote Control host alive per folder listed in a config file.

    ccmorph-rc-hosts [--config PATH]      (run by the com.ccmorph.rc-hosts LaunchAgent)

Config (default $CCMORPH_STATE_DIR/rc-hosts, i.e. ~/.config/ccmorph/rc-hosts):

    route=profile                    # ccmorph profile serving inference for every folder
    ~/project                     # one folder per line
    ~/code/secondary-project route=ds    # a folder may name its own route
    ~/x -- --session-id session_01…   # args after -- go to remote-control (one
                                      # conversation pinned by id)

Each folder gets `ccmorph-rc <route>` (under a pty: remote-control is a terminal
program). A host that exits is relaunched with backoff, and ccmorph-rc resumes the
folder's previous host, so a network drop or 403 costs a restart, not the sessions.
A live edge from a previous run is reused only when it pins the configured tiers;
otherwise it is stopped and replaced -- and only ever by the pid its own record
names, once that process's command line says it is this route's edge.
The config is re-read when it changes: a new folder starts, a removed one stops,
and a changed route restarts that folder's host on the new provider. A changed
`permission=` or a `tier.<alias>=` naming an alias a session actually reads
restarts every host it reaches too -- both are fixed when a host's process
starts, so a restart is the only way they take effect (the shared edge pins the
tier mapping; only folders carry a permission).

A worktree session (`.claude/worktrees/...`) is never pinned: it shares its repo
host's environment, and pinning it takes over that host's row in the app.

A pinned conversation archived in the app stops for good: its bridge exits, the
supervisor finds the archive in the bridge's debug log, adds `archived=<folder>`
and never restarts it (a restart would un-archive it). After idle_days (default
1) without transcript activity its line is commented out and its host is deleted
from the app. Unarchived conversations are never retired: idle only means "not
used lately".

Every active phone session keeps a process, so a message never waits. A session's
process can end while it stays active (archive then unarchive, an idle exit, a
crash), and neither Claude Code nor the server reliably restarts it, so the app
would show it working forever. Every RECOVER_EVERY seconds the supervisor lists
the account's active sessions; one on a repo host's environment that has no
process is handed back to its host -- once that host is up, so the call is not
dropped by a bridge that has not started polling yet -- at most once per
REWAKE_AFTER (bridge/reconnect, the call Claude Code itself uses to re-queue a
session), which spawns it again in a fresh or the kept worktree. The same call
brings back a session whose turn a host restart cut off (recover_interrupted).

An archived session is retired in two steps. After idle_days (default 1) its kept
worktree is removed, with its branch; unarchiving still resumes it, in a fresh
worktree. After purge_days (default 7) the session is deleted from the server and
can no longer be unarchived; its transcript stays on disk. git decides what is
safe: uncommitted changes or unmerged commits keep the worktree, the branch and
the session, and the log says so.
"""
